{"records":[{"id":"pm5v6pwkkg","title":"Genome-wide screens implicate loss of cullin ring ligase 3 in persistent proliferation and genome instability in TP53 deficient cells","description":"TP53 deficiency is the most common alteration in cancer, however, alone is typically insufficient to drive tumorigenesis. To identify genes promoting tumorigenesis in combination with TP53 deficiency, we performed genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screens coupled with proliferation and transformation assays in isogenic cell lines. Loss of several known tumor suppressor genes enhanced cellular proliferation and transformation. Surprisingly, loss neddylation pathway genes, promoted uncontrolled proliferation exclusively in TP53-deficient cells. CUL3 coupled with TP53 loss results in an oncogenic transcriptional program governed by the NFκB, AP-1 and TGF-β pathways. This program maintains persistent cellular proliferation, induces partial epithelial to mesenchymal transition, increases DNA damage, genomic instability, and the rate chromosomal rearrangements. Our findings reveal CUL3 loss as a key event stimulating persistent proliferation in TP53 deficient cells. These findings may be clinically relevant, since TP53-CUL3 deficient cells are highly sensitive to ATM inhibition, exposing a vulnerability that could be exploited for cancer treatment. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-06T17:31:10.704Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pm5v6pwkkg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pm5v6pwkkg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alexandros","last_name":"Drainas","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Cellular Imaging"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9x47wzy8x7","title":"A study of \"Genome-wide CRISPR screens reveal host factors critical for SARS-CoV-2 infection\" by Wei et al. Cell 2020","description":"Identification of host genes essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection may reveal novel therapeutic targets and inform our understanding of COVID-19 pathogenesis. Here, we performed genome-wide CRISPR screens in Vero-E6 cells with SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV, bat coronavirus HKU5 expressing the SARS-CoV-1 spike, and VSV expressing the SARS-CoV-2 spike. Together this identifies potential therapeutic targets for SARS-CoV-2 and reveals SARS-lineage specific and pan-coronavirus host factors that regulate susceptibility to highly pathogenic coronaviruses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-15T21:29:14.06Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9x47wzy8x7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9x47wzy8x7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Craig","last_name":"Wilen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR","Middle East Respiratory Syndrome-Related Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7rgn4p4cvp","title":"PTBP1 enforces ATR-CHK1 signalling determining the potency of CDC7 inhibitors","description":"Background:\nCDC7 kinase is crucial for the initiation of DNA replication and is important for replication stress responses. Several ATP-competitive CDC7 inhibitors (CDC7is) are being developed as possible cancer therapeutics. CDC7 inhibition causes a dose-dependent delay in S-phase progression and mild activation of the S-phase checkpoint pathway, which further limits origin firing through ATR activation.\n\nMethodology:\nTo identify genes that modulate the cellular responses to CDC7is, we established a CRISPR/Cas9 genome-wide loss-of-function screen. The following data represent the closer investigation of one of these genes, PTBP1, using RNA sequencing, flow cytometry, immunofluorescence analysis and additional techniques as described in the manuscript 'PTBP1 enforces ATR-CHK1 signalling determining the potency of CDC7 inhibitors'.\n\nData:\nData represent source data for main figures and supplemental items in support of the manuscript 'PTBP1 enforces ATR-CHK1 signalling determining the potency of CDC7 inhibitors', Göder et al. Data collection, interpretation and findings are addressed in the manuscript.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-05-16T16:30:16.52Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7rgn4p4cvp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7rgn4p4cvp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Anja","last_name":"Göder","orcid":"0000-0001-9743-1656"},{"first_name":"Corrado","last_name":"Santocanale","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"http://dx.doi.org/10.13039/501100001602","name":"Science Foundation Ireland","grant_id":null}],"categories":["DNA Replication","Cell Cycle","Cell Cycle Checkpoint","RNA Splicing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rhsw3nbjhz","title":"Essential gene profiles for human pluripotent stem cells identify uncharacterized genes and substrate dependencies. Mair et al.","description":"Human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) provide an invaluable tool for modeling diseases and hold promise for regenerative medicine. For understanding pluripotency and lineage differentiation mechanisms, a critical first step involves systematically cataloging genes that are indispensable for hPSC maintenance and proliferation. To map genetic determinants of hPSC fitness, we performed genome-scale loss-of-function screens in an inducible Cas9 H1 hPSC line cultured on feeder cells and feeder-free to identify essential genes. Among these, we found FOXH1 and VENTX, genes that encode transcription factors previously implicated in stem cell biology, as well as an uncharacterized gene, C22orf43/DRICH1. hPSCs essential genes are substantially different from other cell lines, and gene essentiality in hPSCs is highly context-dependent with respect to different growth substrates. Our CRISPR screens establish parameters for genome-wide screens in hPSCs, which will facilitate the characterization of unappreciated regulators of hPSC biology to understand the genetic wiring of hPSC fitness and pluripotency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-24T11:57:50.932Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rhsw3nbjhz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rhsw3nbjhz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jelena","last_name":"Tomic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Moffat","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Functional Genomics","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mk32tnmwwt","title":"Multi-Ancestry Whole Genome Sequencing Analysis of Lean Body Mass","description":"Background\nLean body mass is a crucial physiological component of body composition. Although lean body mass has a high heritability, studies evaluating the genetic determinants of lean mass (LM) have to date been limited largely to genome-wide association studies (GWAS) and common variants. Using whole genome sequencing (WGS)-based studies, we aimed to discover novel genetic variants associated with LM in population-based cohorts with multiple ancestries. \nResults \nWe describe the largest WGS-based meta-analysis of lean body mass to date, encompassing 10,729 WGS samples from six TOPMed cohorts and the Louisiana Osteoporosis Study (LOS) cohort, measured with dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry. We identify seven genome-wide loci significantly associated with LM not reported by previous GWAS. We partially replicate these associations in UK Biobank samples. In rare variant analysis, we discover one novel protein-coding gene, DMAC1, associated with both whole-body LM and appendicular LM in females, and a long non-coding RNA gene linked to appendicular LM in males. Both genes exhibit notably high expression levels in skeletal muscle tissue. We investigate the functional roles of two novel lean-mass-related genes, EMP2 and SSUH2, in animal models. EMP2 deficiency in Drosophila leads to significantly reduced mobility without altering muscle tissue or body fat morphology, whereas an SSUH2 gene mutation in zebrafish stimulates muscle fiber growth. \nConclusions \nOur comprehensive analysis, encompassing a large-scale WGS meta-analysis and functional investigations, reveals novel genomic loci and genes associated with lean mass traits, shedding new insights into pathways influencing muscle metabolism and muscle mass regulation.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-02-03T15:00:10.856Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mk32tnmwwt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mk32tnmwwt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xiaoyu","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"KuanJui","last_name":"Su","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yi-Hsiang ","last_name":"Hsu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carolyn J.","last_name":"Crandall","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhe","last_name":"Han","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rebecca D","last_name":"Jackson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Braxton","last_name":"Mitchell","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kerri L","last_name":"Wiggins","orcid":null},{"first_name":"May E.","last_name":"Montasser","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Douglas P","last_name":"Kiel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hong-Wen","last_name":"Deng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ching-Ti","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"David","last_name":"Karasik","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genomics","Muscle","Genotype","Meta-Analysis","Sarcopenia"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9w4g54bv43","title":"Genome-wide screens reveal that resveratrol induces replicative stress in human cells","description":"Resveratrol is a natural product associated with wide-ranging effects in animal and cellular models including lifespan extension. To identify the genetic target of resveratrol in human cells, we conducted genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screens to pinpoint genes that confer sensitivity or resistance to resveratrol. An extensive network of DNA damage response and replicative stress genes exhibited genetic interactions with resveratrol and its analog pterostilbene. These genetic profiles showed similarity to the response to hydroxyurea, an inhibitor of ribonucleotide reductase that causes replicative stress. Resveratrol, pterostilbene and hydroxyurea caused similar depletion of nucleotide pools, inhibition of replication fork progression and induction of replicative stress. The ability of resveratrol to inhibit cell proliferation and S phase transit was independent of the histone deacetylase Sirtuin 1, which has been implicated in lifespan extension by resveratrol. These results establish that a primary impact of resveratrol on human cell proliferation is the induction of low-level replicative stress.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-04T14:56:19.488Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9w4g54bv43","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9w4g54bv43"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yahya","last_name":"Benslimane","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thierry","last_name":"Bertomeu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jasmin","last_name":"Coulombe-Huntington","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mary","last_name":"McQuaid","orcid":null},{"first_name":"María","last_name":"Sánchez-Osuna","orcid":null},{"first_name":"David","last_name":"Papadopoli","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daina","last_name":"Avizonis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mariana","last_name":"De Sa Tavares Russo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Caroline","last_name":"Huard","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ivan","last_name":"Topisirovic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hugo","last_name":"Wurtele","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mike","last_name":"Tyers","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lea","last_name":"Harrington","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Replication","High-Throughput Screening","High-Throughput Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8xhv88c7wt","title":"Genome-wide screening in human kidney organoids identifies developmental and disease-related aspects of nephrogenesis","description":"Human organoids allow the study of proliferation, lineage specification, and three-dimensional tissue development. Here, we present a genome-wide CRISPR screen in iPSC-derived kidney organoids. The combination of inducible genome editing, longitudinal sampling and endpoint sorting of tubular and stromal cells generated a complex, high-quality dataset uncovering a broad spectrum of insightful biology from early development to ‘adult’ epithelial morphogenesis. Our functional dataset allows improving mesoderm induction by ROCK inhibition, contains monogenetic and complex trait kidney disease genes, confirms two additional CAKUT genes (CCDC170 and MYH7B), and provides a large candidate list of ciliopathy-related genes. Finally, the identification of a cis-inhibitory effect of Jagged1 controlling epithelial proliferation shows how mosaic knockouts in pooled CRISPR screening can discover ways of communication between heterogeneous cell populations in complex tissues. Collectively, these data serve both as a rich resource for the kidney community and as a benchmark for future iPSC-derived organoid CRISPR screens.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-29T13:48:59.335Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8xhv88c7wt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8xhv88c7wt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rosemarie","last_name":"Ungricht","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Philipp","last_name":"Hoppe","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f7btcr6x6d","title":"A non-canonical function of glutathione to suppress ferroptosis via FSP1","description":"Whole genome-wide CRISPR KO screen","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-02T11:41:52.925Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f7btcr6x6d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f7btcr6x6d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"BO","last_name":"CHU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5wbzddvhbx","title":"Cisplatin Damaged Gene Loci ","description":"Cisplatin is a classical example of a DNA-targeted genotoxic anticancer drug, yet the exact gene loci attacked by cisplatin as well as the relationship between these gene lesions and action of the drug remain largely unexplored. In this work, we have used a forward chemical genetics method to localize and identify cisplatin damaged gene loci on the whole human genome. By combining affinity separation, high throughput sequencing and mapping to human genome (hg38), we have mapped 14012 protein-encoding genes containing platination lesions, of which 445 genes belong to 628 known gene targets for cancer therapy reported previously. Significantly, cisplatin induces lesions on 306 protein kinase genes with high fold-enrichment during our gene loci mapping, accounting for a remarkable 59% of putative human protein kinase genes. The cisplatin-damaged protein kinase genes are involved in 260 highly associated core-signaling pathways. The data deposited into this database are the mapping results of cisplatin damaged DNA for two biological replicates.     \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-26T18:58:29.8Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5wbzddvhbx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5wbzddvhbx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fuyi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pharmacology","Anticancer Drug"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jsjh28yhfc","title":"Processed RNA-seq gene expression and enrichment analysis data from [Role of NK Cells in Immune Regulation and Sepsis-Induced Inflammation]","description":"Research Hypothesis and Data Description:\n\nThe dataset was generated to investigate the role of NK (natural killer) cells in immune regulation during sepsis-induced inflammation. We hypothesized that NK cells modulate the expression of key immune-related genes and pathways, affecting inflammation and tissue response during sepsis.\n\nData Summary:\n\nThe dataset consists of processed RNA-seq gene expression data from NK cells isolated from control and sepsis models.\n\nIt includes normalized gene expression matrices, lists of differentially expressed genes (DEGs), and functional enrichment analysis results (GO and KEGG pathways).\n\nVisualizations such as heatmaps, barplots, and volcano plots are also included to summarize gene expression patterns and enriched pathways.\n\nNotable Findings:\n\nNK cells in sepsis models show significant changes in genes involved in cytokine signaling, immune response, and inflammatory pathways.\n\nEnrichment analysis highlights key pathways potentially regulated by NK cells, such as TNF signaling, NF-κB pathway, and interferon-mediated responses.\nThe data suggests that NK cells may have both pro- and anti-inflammatory roles in sepsis progression, providing insights into immune modulation mechanisms.\n\nData Interpretation and Use:\n\nResearchers can use this dataset to explore NK cell-specific transcriptional changes, identify novel targets for immunomodulation, or perform comparative analysis with other immune cell types.\nThe processed gene expression and enrichment results allow reanalysis, integration with other datasets, and functional interpretation without needing access to raw sequencing files.\nThis dataset is particularly useful for studying immune regulation, inflammation, and cell-specific responses in sepsis models.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-30T16:33:49.279Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jsjh28yhfc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jsjh28yhfc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Aneela","last_name":"Bashir","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Muhammad Asif","last_name":"Mrza","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Immunology","Life Sciences","Genomics","Bioinformatics","Transcriptomics","Computational Biology"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":127134,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":167,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":127134,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":126547,"restricted":66,"embargoed":521},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2173},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":667},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":448},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":409},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":407},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":396},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":386},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":312},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":253},{"id":"https://ror.org/05r0vyz12","name":"Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades","count":216}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":124602},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1821},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":182},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":130},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":73},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":53},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":37},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":26}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":483},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":405},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":358},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":331},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":293}]}}